\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Dirichlet-kernel magnitude $|D(\delta )| = |\sin (\pi \delta ) / (N\sin (\pi \delta / N))|$ from \eqref  {eq:dirichlet} at $N= 1024$, plotted against the integer-Doppler offset $\delta $. Two cases are overlaid: the integer-aligned residual ($\kappa = k_p^{\text  {real}} - k_p = 0$, blue), where the kernel collapses to a sampling indicator and an integer-Doppler dictionary loses no per-path energy; and the worst-case fractional residual ($|\kappa | = 0.5$, red), where the integer-bin samples (red circles) at $\delta \in \{0, +1\}$ each carry $\approx 41\%$ of per-path energy and the samples at $\delta \in \{-1, +2\}$ each carry $\approx 4.4\%$. The dashed vertical lines mark the $k_{\mathrm  {leak}}= 2$ truncation boundary used by the simulator; the two-sided sum of squared truncated coefficients beyond $|\delta | > k_{\mathrm  {leak}}$ amounts to $\approx 8.3\%$ of per-path energy at $|\kappa | = 0.5$, the figure quoted in the paragraph above. The non-zero side-lobes flanking the kernel peak are the structurally predictable energy that the post-OMP virtual-tap expansion (\S  \ref {ssec:joint}) and the learned support-refinement ranker (\S  \ref {ssec:c2rel}) are designed to recover.}}{10}{figure.caption.16}\protected@file@percent }
\providecommand*\caption@xref[2]{\@setref\relax\@undefined{#1}}
\newlabel{fig:dirichlet}{{1}{10}{Dirichlet-kernel magnitude $|D(\delta )| = |\sin (\pi \delta ) / (\Nsub \sin (\pi \delta / \Nsub ))|$ from \eqref {eq:dirichlet} at $\Nsub = 1024$, plotted against the integer-Doppler offset $\delta $. Two cases are overlaid: the integer-aligned residual ($\kappa = k_p^{\text {real}} - k_p = 0$, blue), where the kernel collapses to a sampling indicator and an integer-Doppler dictionary loses no per-path energy; and the worst-case fractional residual ($|\kappa | = 0.5$, red), where the integer-bin samples (red circles) at $\delta \in \{0, +1\}$ each carry $\approx 41\%$ of per-path energy and the samples at $\delta \in \{-1, +2\}$ each carry $\approx 4.4\%$. The dashed vertical lines mark the $\kleak = 2$ truncation boundary used by the simulator; the two-sided sum of squared truncated coefficients beyond $|\delta | > \kleak $ amounts to $\approx 8.3\%$ of per-path energy at $|\kappa | = 0.5$, the figure quoted in the paragraph above. The non-zero side-lobes flanking the kernel peak are the structurally predictable energy that the post-OMP virtual-tap expansion (\S \ref {ssec:joint}) and the learned support-refinement ranker (\S \ref {ssec:c2rel}) are designed to recover}{figure.caption.16}{}}
